It was also led by the Postgress creator professor Mike Stonereaker and the spark creator and database co-founder Matezah.

22
00:01:43,439 --> 00:01:50,870
So during the research project, we really tested the capability of databases and see how databases can help

23
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:58,630
you create reliable programs and how databases can help you make your programs more observable and debugable.

24
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:09,510
So during the research project, we built several prototypes and we wrote several papers and when we presented it, people are really excited about the capability that deboss can bring.

25
00:02:09,510 --> 00:02:12,869
So when we graduated in 2023,

26
00:02:12,869 --> 00:02:21,270
we decided to based on the research project co-ound deboss and deboss as a company right now we focus on building durable software

27
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:28,229
and we believe that all software should be reliable and observable and scalable by default.

28
00:02:28,229 --> 00:02:32,869
So now deboss stands for durable backends observable and scalable.

29
00:02:32,869 --> 00:02:40,070
So you mentioned the use of databases for basically running your applications but why is that a new concept?

30
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:44,790
Everybody uses databases for running apps or most people do.

31
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:48,949
So what is the difference here? What is the secret sauce that you talking about?

32
00:02:48,959 --> 00:02:51,750
Yeah.

33
00:02:51,750 --> 00:03:00,710
So it's true that people have been storing their business critical data in databases for like 30 40 years but the new concept is

34
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:09,589
that we also want to persist programs execution state in the database like here's your program and it has multiple steps

35
00:03:09,589 --> 00:03:22,869
and we want to persist the steps output and the input into the database so that if your program crashes or machine failed we'll be able to resume from exactly where you left off.

36
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:30,149
So the idea is to in addition to your application data, we also store your program execution state in the database.

37
00:03:30,149 --> 00:03:42,070
Especially if you're having long running and dynamic workflows in your programs, you really don't want to restart from scratch every time you hit a system error or you have a machine failure.

38
00:03:42,070 --> 00:03:50,230
So Chan, maybe you can explain to the listeners what exactly do you define a workflow as opposed to a service? Yeah.

39
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:55,110
So to get started I think we can talk about what is a workflow in this context.

40
00:03:55,110 --> 00:03:57,750
So traditionally people think workflows as state machines.

41
00:03:57,750 --> 00:03:59,910
You have to define a DAC stuff like that.

42
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:04,869
But actually in deboss anything can be a workflow.

43
00:04:04,869 --> 00:04:12,309
So to give a concrete example a workflow is a sequence of operations or function calls.

44
00:04:12,309 --> 00:04:18,069
So a very typical example in like durable execution is let's say checkout service.

45
00:04:18,069 --> 00:04:32,150
So if you're implementing a checkout service usually you have to call say reserve inventory you have to update a database to make sure that you have enough inventory and then after that if you successfully reserve the inventory you will cut out

46
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:43,110
to the payment process for example this could be an external service like stripe or PayPal you will say I want to charge a user this much

47
00:04:43,110 --> 00:04:51,350
and after that you need to wait for the response from those services and then based on the result you will decide whether to fulfill the order

48
00:04:51,350 --> 00:05:02,550
and send a confirmation email to the user or you have to undo your reservation for the inventory and then cancel the order and send a cancellation email.

49
00:05:02,550 --> 00:05:07,029
So this process can be abstracted as a workflow and then like what guarantees do we want for this workflow?

50
00:05:07,039 --> 00:05:15,510
First we want to make sure that once I click that check out button all steps will eventually succeed or complete.

51
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:18,150
Right?

52
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:24,790
I don't want to say I pay for my process but I never receive my item or I reserve the inventory but never charge the user.

53
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:30,390
So that's the first guarantee and the second guarantee is that I want to guarantee effectively exactly once.

54
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:38,230
So if I charge a user I want to charge them once and if I reserve the inventory I also want to only reserve it once.

55
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:42,230
So you talked about workflow and you mentioned transaction.

56
00:05:42,230 --> 00:05:45,189
What's the relationship?

57
00:05:45,189 --> 00:05:59,749
So in this context a transaction is essentially interaction to a local database and then a workflow can be composed of transactions and also external services or we just call it steps in general.

58
00:05:59,749 --> 00:06:14,710
So in deboss a workflow is a sequence of steps and some steps could be transactions that talk to a database and other normal steps well normal steps can be any functions right can be talking

59
00:06:14,710 --> 00:06:22,870
to external APIs or can have some any non-deterministic factors operations in that step but overall the workflow needs

60
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:37,670
to be deterministic and determinism and item potency are two foundations for durable workflows and essentially What we build is a library that you can just easily install it and then add annotations to your program.

61
00:06:37,670 --> 00:06:42,070
So I described the checkout workflow to make it durable in deboss.

62
00:06:42,070 --> 00:06:55,029
You essentially put annotation at deboss.workflow in front of the overall orchestration workflow function and then you put at step at the function definition of every individual step.

63
00:06:55,039 --> 00:07:04,309
Then when we call that function essentially we'll first we basically put wrappers around those functions to make them durable.

64
00:07:04,309 --> 00:07:17,350
So to make it more concrete basically when you start a workflow when you start call the workflow function the wrapper would first checkpoint the input of the workflow in the database

65
00:07:17,350 --> 00:07:23,189
and then for each step we first check have we executed this step before.

66
00:07:23,189 --> 00:07:30,390
If so, we directly read the recorded output from the database and return the output instead of re-executing it.

67
00:07:30,390 --> 00:07:33,909
So this way we guarantee exactly once.

68
00:07:33,909 --> 00:07:41,110
So the thing is for external systems the way to guarantee exactly once is to guarantee at least once plus item potency key

69
00:07:41,110 --> 00:07:49,510
and deboss basically automatically generates an item potency key per workflow and per step.

70
00:07:49,510 --> 00:08:02,150
So we can guarantee that each step will execute effectively exactly once and then after each step finishes we'll checkpoint the output into the database and so on so forth until we reach the end of the workflow

71
00:08:02,150 --> 00:08:11,270
and say if something crashes in the middle when we recover the deboss library will look into the database and see which workflow is still pending.

72
00:08:11,270 --> 00:08:18,390
And then if the workflow is still pending, we'll just essentially replay the workflow from the start and walk through all the steps.

73
00:08:18,390 --> 00:08:23,189
And then for the finished steps, we'll already see the database record.

74
00:08:23,189 --> 00:08:25,670
And then we'll skip those steps.

75
00:08:25,670 --> 00:08:33,909
And then we'll eventually lend to the last completed step and then we'll continue resume the workflow from where it left off.

76
00:08:33,909 --> 00:08:36,469
So that's basically a highle idea of how deboss.

77
00:08:36,479 --> 00:08:38,870
You mentioned the item potency key.

78
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:40,389
explain that further.

79
00:08:40,399 --> 00:08:44,310
Yeah.

80
00:08:44,310 --> 00:08:50,710
So essentially item potency key is a way to guarantee that each step is executed once and only once.

81
00:08:50,710 --> 00:08:58,230
So essentially when you work with external API say stripe payment right you want to say

82
00:08:58,230 --> 00:09:05,990
I only want to charge it this user once and then you can pick it back a item potency key in your request to stripe.

83
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:20,230
So if Stripe received the same key again, it will not issue another invoice to the user, Stripe will be able to look up its own database and say, "Hey, I already have an outstanding invoice for this checkout session." So they will only charge a user once.

84
00:09:20,230 --> 00:09:25,430
So I think item potency is a very important concept in terms of durable execution.

85
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:31,990
And in Deboss, we automatically generate an item potency key for a workflow if you don't already provide one.

86
00:09:31,990 --> 00:09:35,910
And then for each step we'll basically append the step sequence number to it.

87
00:09:35,910 --> 00:09:40,949
So every step in the workflow will also be uniquely identified.

88
00:09:40,949 --> 00:09:52,070
So the reason we need item potency key is that you can't really control external systems right say even if we only call stripe API once.

89
00:09:52,070 --> 00:09:59,269
We don't know whether the network connection will fail or maybe stripe will fail or there's some intermediate transient errors that may happen.

90
00:09:59,269 --> 00:10:11,030
So like if we don't get the result back, we may think it failed, but in order to make sure that the checkout goes through, we may want to retry it and we retry it.

91
00:10:11,030 --> 00:10:18,949
We can't generate a new session because maybe the stripe payment already succeeded, but it just the network was cut when Stripe tried to send the result back.

92
00:10:18,949 --> 00:10:21,269
So we have to retry it.

93
00:10:21,269 --> 00:10:28,470
And when we retry it, the way to tell Stripe that this is the same checkout request I sent it before is to append the item potency key.

94
00:10:28,470 --> 00:10:35,910
So item policy key is the key to guarantee correctness and exactly once execution when you work with external systems.

95
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:40,389
Could you talk more about the deboss architecture?

96
00:10:40,389 --> 00:10:42,790
You did talk about the workflow and the step.

97
00:10:42,790 --> 00:10:51,990
Is there any other key elements of the architecture you'd like people to know about and especially if they have origins in the research that you had done in the past?

98
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,870
Yeah.

99
00:10:54,870 --> 00:11:00,150
So deboss workflows and steps and transactions were implemented directly as SQL interactions in the library.

100
00:11:00,150 --> 00:11:06,069
And then beyond that we also have several other primitives.

101
00:11:06,069 --> 00:11:08,550
The one is widely used is called deboss cues.

102
00:11:08,550 --> 00:11:11,829
So we also implement cues on top of postgress.

103
00:11:11,829 --> 00:11:16,949
With cues basically you will be able to group.

104
00:11:16,949 --> 00:11:25,190
So instead of directly invoking or executing a workflow synchronously, you can incue a workflow and then other workers will be able to pick it up.

105
00:11:25,190 --> 00:11:31,509
So the Q is also just a database record, right?

106
00:11:31,509 --> 00:11:37,670
We just inq by saying here's the task, here's a Q name and then for the worker they can just pull from each Q.

107
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:45,750
So the benefit of Q is that it makes it really easy to control concurrency and rate limiting.

108
00:11:45,750 --> 00:11:56,710
Say for example we can have different cues like open AIQ, cloud Q, stuff like that and we want to say for the open AIQ we only want to send the request five times per minute.

109
00:11:56,710 --> 00:12:04,389
So this is a way to group your invocations and to control how many outstanding requests you send to external systems.

110
00:12:04,399 --> 00:12:09,670
And another thing is based on research is messaging system.

111
00:12:09,670 --> 00:12:22,389
So when workflows and steps basically they are all within the deboss territory but when you need to interact with external systems you need a way to communicate with the workflow.

112
00:12:22,389 --> 00:12:31,030
So give an example of this so say actually the checkout workflow right when you call stripe you don't block and wait until user to pay out.

113
00:12:31,030 --> 00:12:37,509
So what you actually do is that Stripe will directly return a confirmation saying okay we are processing your payment

114
00:12:37,509 --> 00:12:46,069
but when your payment is done we'll send you a web hook invocation to say this payment is done

115
00:12:46,069 --> 00:12:54,389
and now how to do it in deboss is that so in a workflow we can have a deboss receive on a specific topic waiting

116
00:12:54,389 --> 00:13:08,470
for the specific stripe payment session and then we can implement a web hook that listens Stripe call back when a stripe call back we can extract because as we mentioned we have the item policy key we can uniquely identify which workflow we should call back to

117
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:20,310
and then we will be able to send say deboss send with that item potency key and then send stripe payments result either paid

118
00:13:20,310 --> 00:13:33,269
or payment rejected stuff like that and then the workflow well using the receive will be able to get that information so this is really useful if you want to like I think almost all the payment system

119
00:13:33,269 --> 00:13:40,870
for example is using this type of pattern and also if you want anything that goes to like human in the loop say you want

120
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:50,870
to send a human verification email and then you want to wait for the human confirmation to come back you can't just block there

121
00:13:50,870 --> 00:14:03,189
and wait you have to wait for a call back and then deboss send and receive primitive allows you to do that and this library is what you call as deboss transact.

122
00:14:03,189 --> 00:14:05,991
Anybody can use it as long as they annotate their code. Yes.

123
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:11,031
So deboss transact is an open source library. It's MIT license.

124
00:14:11,031 --> 00:14:13,110
So anyone will be able to use it.

125
00:14:13,110 --> 00:14:16,150
It's currently available in Typescript and Python.

126
00:14:16,150 --> 00:14:18,790
So we are adding more language support.

127
00:14:18,790 --> 00:14:27,110
So for the audience if you have any feedback on what languages you wish to see, please contact us and we'll add more.

128
00:14:27,110 --> 00:14:29,430
So for the transact library you can install it and run it anywhere.

129
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:31,430
What is the deboss cloud?

130
00:14:31,440 --> 00:14:34,389
Right.

131
00:14:34,389 --> 00:14:39,030
So deboss cloud is a serverless hosting platform for transact applications.

132
00:14:39,030 --> 00:14:43,750
So say so you can run your transact applications anywhere on your own laptop or on a kubernetes cluster.

133
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:51,269
But if you don't have any resources and if you don't want to manage any clusters you can deploy your app to deboss cloud.

134
00:14:51,279 --> 00:14:57,350
So if I do use deboss transact library I must get a Postgress can I if I wanted

135
00:14:57,360 --> 00:15:07,990
to use another database system could I currently deboss transact supports any Postgress compatible databases so you can use it

136
00:15:07,990 --> 00:15:20,710
with your own Postgress server like many of our users just simply add deboss transact because they already have a Postgress server so they just use the same server to store your execution state data

137
00:15:20,710 --> 00:15:25,910
but you can also use other offerings like each cloud provider has some Postgress offering and

138
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:36,629
we also compatible with new serverless Postgress like neon or superbase or cockroach db or yugabet db there are a lot of options here

139
00:15:36,629 --> 00:15:45,590
so is that just because that's the database you tested with or is there any specific features of postgress postcress compatible databases

140
00:15:45,590 --> 00:15:54,710
that you leverage yeah I guess it goes back to the question of why do we choose postgress So like there are a couple of reasons we chose Postgress.

141
00:15:54,710 --> 00:15:57,670
First is that the ecosystem is huge.

142
00:15:57,670 --> 00:16:02,949
As I said there are a lot of providers in the cloud or there are a lot of on-prem solutions as well.

143
00:16:02,959 --> 00:16:05,189
People know how to operate Postgress.

144
00:16:05,199 --> 00:16:11,671
It's a very mature and battle tested technology and people really trust Postgress. So that's one reason.

145
00:16:11,671 --> 00:16:15,350
And the second reason is that it's a relational database.

146
00:16:15,350 --> 00:16:18,311
So it has built-in transactions. It's very reliable.

147
00:16:18,311 --> 00:16:25,910
So we don't need to worry about like it has backups and it has replication if you want to.

148
00:16:25,910 --> 00:16:28,629
And finally the extension ecosystem is great.

149
00:16:28,629 --> 00:16:33,990
Some of our users actually use Postgress as a vector store.

150
00:16:33,990 --> 00:16:35,910
So some people also use Postgress to store time series data.

151
00:16:35,910 --> 00:16:39,590
So really like with a single database you can achieve a lot of things.

152
00:16:39,590 --> 00:16:44,230
You can store basically all data from transactional data to analytics data to vector data.

153
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:48,550
So we really like this versality of Postgress.

154
00:16:48,550 --> 00:16:56,870
So Chen, a lot of our listeners I think are really interested in the how like peering behind the black box.

155
00:16:56,870 --> 00:17:07,190
So if they will have access to the Postgress database where the state is getting stored if they peak in there what will they see and where should they peak? Yeah.

156
00:17:07,190 --> 00:17:11,750
So Debb stores all the information in the separate logical database inside the Postgress server.

157
00:17:11,750 --> 00:17:19,110
So you can think of a logical database is another namespace within the Postgress server

158
00:17:19,110 --> 00:17:26,870
to isolate it from your main application database so that we don't interrupt or we don't disturb your normal tables or your normal queries.

159
00:17:26,870 --> 00:17:33,830
So in that database under the deboss schema you will be able to find several tables that stores information.

160
00:17:33,830 --> 00:17:35,350
So there are several tables you want to look into.

161
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:37,669
The first is workflow status table.

162
00:17:37,679 --> 00:17:42,710
That's the core table that stores workflow information.

163
00:17:42,710 --> 00:17:47,350
And then when you first execute a workflow, it will say workflow pending.

164
00:17:47,350 --> 00:17:52,230
And then as your workflow progress and eventually succeed or fail,

165
00:17:52,230 --> 00:18:00,710
we'll update the workflow status to error with the error the actual error or success with the actual output.

166
00:18:00,710 --> 00:18:04,870
So that table is the core that drives the deboss that stores the state changes of deboss.

167
00:18:04,870 --> 00:18:09,190
And then the second table you want to look at is operation outputs table.

168
00:18:09,190 --> 00:18:13,750
In that table, that's where we store the serialized output of each step.

169
00:18:13,750 --> 00:18:22,230
And by looking into that table, you will see the workflow ID and then the step ID inside the workflow and the result of every single step.

170
00:18:22,230 --> 00:18:31,990
So by looking at those two tables, you can piece together what workflow has executed when and what steps has executed at what time stamp.

171
00:18:31,990 --> 00:18:36,870
Besides that, we also have the workflow cues table.

172
00:18:36,870 --> 00:18:39,590
The Q's table basically groups workflows into different cues.

173
00:18:39,590 --> 00:18:45,590
And then by saying different Q's, it's just we assign a different name in the Q names table.

174
00:18:45,590 --> 00:18:51,190
And that will allow us to quickly look up what workflows are assigned to be executed in that queue.

175
00:18:51,190 --> 00:18:54,150
So I would say the real beauty is in SQL.

176
00:18:54,150 --> 00:19:01,510
You can use SQL to manage your workflows and you can also use SQL to simply query them and observe what happened in your system.

177
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:07,110
Do you provide out of the box visualizations for what you see in these tables?

178
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:10,390
Yes.

179
00:19:10,390 --> 00:19:23,350
So if you log into deboss console like console.debos.dev you'll be able to see we have table visualization you can basically select or filter based on the workflow names or based on workflow status.

180
00:19:23,350 --> 00:19:29,590
And we are also actively developing a graph visualization of the workflow execution graph.

181
00:19:29,590 --> 00:19:38,390
So say what workflow has started and how many steps it has finished and like what's a workflow parent child relationship between different workflows.

182
00:19:38,390 --> 00:19:42,310
Say like one workflow can invoke subworkflow that execute other tasks.

183
00:19:42,310 --> 00:19:46,789
So you'll be able to connect the dots by looking at that graph.

184
00:19:46,789 --> 00:19:59,110
Chan, do you have any real world example where maybe talking to your customers where unreliable workflow executions cause them really problematic system failures or inefficiencies?

185
00:19:59,120 --> 00:20:01,909
Yeah.

186
00:20:01,909 --> 00:20:04,630
So let's see there are several use cases.

187
00:20:04,630 --> 00:20:10,230
My favorite one is that one of our customers have to persist data across multiple systems.

188
00:20:10,230 --> 00:20:16,470
Say Shopify sends some data through Kafka and for each message each message contains some customer data.

189
00:20:16,470 --> 00:20:18,310
They want to persist in their local Postgress database.

190
00:20:18,310 --> 00:20:21,029
They want to persist in their CRM.

191
00:20:21,029 --> 00:20:23,270
They're want to persist in their ERP system.

192
00:20:23,270 --> 00:20:29,029
So they want to make sure that the data is consistent across all systems.

193
00:20:29,029 --> 00:20:31,750
So they chose Deboss.

194
00:20:31,750 --> 00:20:36,230
And if you don't have correct customer data, you can probably lose customers.

195
00:20:36,230 --> 00:20:38,310
So that's what they really don't want to see.

196
00:20:38,310 --> 00:20:48,070
And deboss guarantees that whenever you receive a message from Kafka, we guarantee that the message will appear across all systems.

197
00:20:48,070 --> 00:20:58,070
Maybe that answers one of my questions, which was why would I not roll my own or use code generators to help me write the code for durable execution.

198
00:20:58,080 --> 00:21:07,831
I think one of what you say tells me that there's a lot of guarantees and compliance that using deboss might help me with. Is that fair?

199
00:21:07,831 --> 00:21:11,590
Are you going through some kind of compliance process that people can leverage?

200
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:13,990
Yeah.

201
00:21:13,990 --> 00:21:16,789
So I think we can talk a bit more about the observability angle of deboss.

202
00:21:16,799 --> 00:21:24,470
So because everything is stored in Postgress, it's really easy to query and visualize what's going on.

203
00:21:24,470 --> 00:21:30,870
So my favorite quote from customers is that they said deboss is great because everything is in Postgress.

204
00:21:30,870 --> 00:21:39,909
I can just use SQL queries to see what workflows have run, what workflows failed and what happened at each step.

205
00:21:39,909 --> 00:21:41,669
And based on the information we store in Postgress,

206
00:21:41,669 --> 00:21:52,149
we are also developing more observability features like graph visualization of the workflow and its steps and the workflow maybe spawning multiple workflows there.

207
00:21:52,159 --> 00:22:02,549
So we are visualizing the parent child relationship there and more than that what we can provide is actually management over your workflows.

208
00:22:02,549 --> 00:22:12,630
So because those are just database records if you want to say resume a workflow you can just reincue it put it back into a Q table.

209
00:22:12,630 --> 00:22:19,190
If you want to cancel workflow then you can just mark the workflow as canled and then the downstream process will just cancel the operation.

210
00:22:19,190 --> 00:22:25,990
And if you want to say, we can talk more about it later when we talk about operations with deboss,

211
00:22:25,990 --> 00:22:29,669
but like if you want to restart a workflow with your new versions of a code,

212
00:22:29,669 --> 00:22:38,549
you can just copy the workflow input information and assign it with a new item potency key and then just start execution on the new version.

213
00:22:38,549 --> 00:22:45,909
And because data is in Postgress is a relational database is structured data and structured data is really easy to analyze and to observe.

214
00:22:45,909 --> 00:22:48,549
A little bit of segue here and would like

215
00:22:48,559 --> 00:23:01,830
to contrast deboss with solutions already in the market and I did mention our earlier episode the latest one being with Maxim Fativ on temporal.

216
00:23:01,830 --> 00:23:13,991
How does deboss contrast with temporal and is your definition of workflow consistent with temporal's definition of workflow and durable execution? Yeah.

217
00:23:13,991 --> 00:23:18,870
So that's a good question and we got asked about that question a lot.

218
00:23:18,870 --> 00:23:31,190
So I think the workflow definition in deboss and temporal are essentially the same but the core difference is how we perceive the implementation of this durable execution.

219
00:23:31,190 --> 00:23:33,430
So in temporal we call the pattern external orchestration pattern.

220
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:37,990
With external orchestration which means you have to start a temporal server and

221
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:48,549
then when you run your workflow instead of directly executing each step your workflow function will need to incue a step into a temporal worker

222
00:23:48,549 --> 00:23:59,191
and then temporal worker temporal server will instead push the notification or will instead notify a worker node to process that. They call it activity.

223
00:23:59,191 --> 00:24:02,149
We call it a step.

224
00:24:02,149 --> 00:24:13,909
And then after the worker node finishes a step or activity, they will send it back to temporal server and temporal server will persist the result and then send it back to the main workflow function.

225
00:24:13,909 --> 00:24:26,230
So basically for every invocation of your step there are multiple network hops and then by contrast in deboss we essentially embed dup execution in your program.

226
00:24:26,230 --> 00:24:36,549
So when you call the workflow it's just a function call and when a workflow calls a step it's again it's a function call but a function call is intercept by the deboss library.

227
00:24:36,549 --> 00:24:38,870
So everything will happen just in your program.

228
00:24:38,870 --> 00:24:43,990
So we believe the benefit of deboss is that it's really simple.

229
00:24:43,990 --> 00:24:46,549
All you need is your program and your postgress database.

230
00:24:46,549 --> 00:24:55,669
You don't have to deploy an extra orchestration server and you don't have to deploy like distributed workers to process your steps.

231
00:24:55,669 --> 00:25:04,310
I get the part about not having to deploy an orchestration server but then how do you achieve orchestration?

232
00:25:04,310 --> 00:25:07,990
So deboss implements job execution this as orchestration.

233
00:25:07,990 --> 00:25:15,110
So just to clarify that we achieve job execution by intercepting the function calls.

234
00:25:15,110 --> 00:25:30,070
So you can think of it as when you call a deboss decorated function instead of directly executing the function we wrap around the workflow function for example to say we first persist the input and then call the function.

235
00:25:30,070 --> 00:25:38,230
When execute the workflow function the workflow function will call each step and again each step is also wrapped by deboss.

236
00:25:38,230 --> 00:25:46,870
So in the wrapper deboss will first check if the step has finished before if not execute it persist the result

237
00:25:46,870 --> 00:25:53,909
if it has been executed before directly return a result so everything happens in the language layer

238
00:25:53,909 --> 00:26:08,549
so that's why we don't need to send it over to a separate worker to a separate message cue to achieve this is there any other areas where it's important to compare and contrast with temporal

239
00:26:08,549 --> 00:26:23,350
I think The two main area one is simplicity like in order to add deboss to your program it's really simple you install it as a library add to your existing program by decorating your functions and you're done and then to implement something temporal you have

240
00:26:23,360 --> 00:26:34,950
to basically restructure program into like you have to think in a distributed system way every time when you cut out a step it is essentially an RPC call

241
00:26:34,950 --> 00:26:42,390
to another worker and then to executed and and then wait for the result to come back.

242
00:26:42,390 --> 00:26:48,070
So we think simplicity is the number one differentiator and then it's also very simple to operate.

243
00:26:48,070 --> 00:26:57,430
So say like to run deboss in production all you need is a Postgress server and then basically people already know how to operate Postgress servers.

244
00:26:57,430 --> 00:26:59,830
So we don't add much of the operational overhead to it.

245
00:26:59,830 --> 00:27:08,950
By contrast, if you use any like external orchestration services, you have to host their service or you have to rely on their cloud providers cloud offerings.

246
00:27:08,960 --> 00:27:22,550
For example, every time you want to call a step, you have to communicate to their cloud and say, I want to execute a step and their cloud will send a message to worker to execute it. So on so forth.

247
00:27:22,550 --> 00:27:27,510
So that will also give some like implication to performance, right?

248
00:27:27,510 --> 00:27:39,669
In deboss every step is we'll add basically add a database right which is like a few milliseconds while if you do everything over the network that will easily go a few hundred a millisecond.

249
00:27:39,679 --> 00:27:49,750
Other than the simplicity of use do you have any thoughts about when a developer should when they need durable execution they should consider deboss versus temporal?

250
00:27:49,760 --> 00:27:56,710
Yeah I mean temporal is really great technology like it's used by a lot of companies.

251
00:27:56,710 --> 00:28:08,310
I think one benefit of the temporal model is that if you want to have a workflow that's consisted of different steps or written different languages, it could be easier to use the temporal model.

252
00:28:08,310 --> 00:28:15,029
Say you have a workflow written in Python, but maybe some steps are written in Go, other in Java and Rust.

253
00:28:15,029 --> 00:28:19,350
If you have such heterogeneous workflow, it will currently be easier to do it in temporal.

254
00:28:19,350 --> 00:28:27,510
Well, on the other hand, if your program is just in Python or TypeScript, it will definitely be easier to do in Deboss.

255
00:28:27,510 --> 00:28:32,549
In fact, like a current trend is we call full stack applications.

256
00:28:32,549 --> 00:28:38,950
So, when users write their front end code in Typescript, they actually also want to write their backend in Typescript.

257
00:28:38,950 --> 00:28:42,789
So, that's why like deboss in this case makes more sense because it's very lightweight.

258
00:28:42,789 --> 00:28:48,149
You just add it as another uh TypeScript library and then use it in a program.

259
00:28:48,159 --> 00:28:56,870
How much of this comparison also applies to some of the other technologies like AWS step functions?

260
00:28:56,870 --> 00:28:59,029
Yeah, so the external orchestration part is the same.

261
00:28:59,039 --> 00:29:09,430
So we actually had some performance benchmarks against step functions and we found that for deboss we are like each step is like a few milliseconds

262
00:29:09,430 --> 00:29:17,990
whereas in step functions every time you have to schedule a step it will push to a que and then you have to dq and wait for result.

263
00:29:17,990 --> 00:29:20,230
So it will come back as like 200 milliseconds.

264
00:29:20,230 --> 00:29:22,549
So the performance gap is pretty large.

265
00:29:22,549 --> 00:29:29,830
And another difference is that step functions requires you to use a late JSON description language like

266
00:29:29,830 --> 00:29:38,149
to ident to basically specify the DAC and that could be another overhead I would say.

267
00:29:38,149 --> 00:29:40,230
Well though like step functions also provide a graphical interface.

268
00:29:40,230 --> 00:29:45,830
You can drag and drop your workflows into a DAC.

269
00:29:45,830 --> 00:29:53,190
This is really nice and this can be used by like for example developers or people without too much coding experience.

270
00:29:53,190 --> 00:29:57,909
However, the problem is that it's very easy to use when you have simple tasks.

271
00:29:57,909 --> 00:30:00,549
But when you have a more complicated task, it's hard to manage.

272
00:30:00,559 --> 00:30:04,870
Just share an anecdote from our conversations with users.

273
00:30:04,870 --> 00:30:11,430
someone was switching from step functions to deboss because they said eventually they gave up on code review

274
00:30:11,430 --> 00:30:21,510
because they have to review 3,000 lines of JSON code and then in that case using deboss is better because all your workflow logic is essentially just your code.

275
00:30:21,510 --> 00:30:30,710
So you write workflows as code and then you can do code review, you can do your debugging normally as you would when you develop your functions.

276
00:30:30,710 --> 00:30:33,110
A follow-up question there.

277
00:30:33,110 --> 00:30:40,230
In some of the previous episodes on this topic, there was pros and cons of the graphical representation discussed.

278
00:30:40,230 --> 00:30:50,149
Certainly, there was a sentiment expressed that developers are not very fond of the graphical representation, but more business users find it useful.

279
00:30:50,149 --> 00:30:53,830
What is your experience there?

280
00:30:53,830 --> 00:31:00,790
And if I did want to communicate that with a business user and I was as a developer using deboss, what are my options? Yeah, that's a great question.

281
00:31:00,799 --> 00:31:05,669
I think I have the same experience when talking to uh developers.

282
00:31:05,669 --> 00:31:07,750
They usually say they want code.

283
00:31:07,750 --> 00:31:09,750
They want to see code.

284
00:31:09,750 --> 00:31:14,789
But when we talk to more business focused people, they want to see like what's going on.

285
00:31:14,789 --> 00:31:23,430
Like say they want to have a visual or a graph visualization of what happened like how many steps happened,

286
00:31:23,430 --> 00:31:28,389
how many workflows happened and if something failed, which step failed.

287
00:31:28,389 --> 00:31:42,630
So in deboss we basically provide we are actually actively developing a graphical visualization based on the information stored in database and the reason is that so you can't define workflows based on graph

288
00:31:42,640 --> 00:31:50,149
but we provide observability into what happened what have executed before I think that is a good balance I'd say

289
00:31:50,159 --> 00:32:02,710
because developers usually use code to develop their workflows well Business focused people usually need to see what happened like the execution of those workflows. I think that's a good combination.

290
00:32:02,720 --> 00:32:07,990
You had talked earlier about scheduling how one would achieve that.

291
00:32:07,990 --> 00:32:11,110
Can you talk about roll back with deboss?

292
00:32:11,110 --> 00:32:18,070
Because roll back was another big plus of a workflow system or be able to achieve that.

293
00:32:18,070 --> 00:32:20,310
How would I as a developer achieve that if I was using deboss?

294
00:32:20,320 --> 00:32:23,110
Yeah.

295
00:32:23,110 --> 00:32:31,190
So if you use deboss, you can just handle exceptions normally as you would when you develop your API or your service.

296
00:32:31,190 --> 00:32:36,630
So in the checkout example, so if I receive if the workflow says sees the payment was failed,

297
00:32:36,630 --> 00:32:43,750
then you will need to call the undo inventory reservation function and it also need to call some functions to cancel the order.

298
00:32:43,760 --> 00:32:51,909
So in deboss you basically express them in code as error like if you see this error call those sequence of functions.

299
00:32:51,919 --> 00:32:57,750
If you see other types of error do something else.

300
00:32:57,750 --> 00:33:02,630
So we do ask users to explicitly specify the roll back actions.

301
00:33:02,630 --> 00:33:04,549
And the roll back actions are also steps.

302
00:33:04,549 --> 00:33:11,509
So the benefit of using deboss is that we'll guarantee everything the workflow that all the steps will run to completion.

303
00:33:11,509 --> 00:33:20,231
How do I as a developer take an existing body of code and the annotations that debus wants? What process would I go through?

304
00:33:20,240 --> 00:33:23,190
Yeah, so we think it's pretty easy.

305
00:33:23,190 --> 00:33:25,590
So you first install the library.

306
00:33:25,590 --> 00:33:28,149
Uh if you use Python, it's pip install.

307
00:33:28,149 --> 00:33:30,630
If you use hyperscript, it's npm install.

308
00:33:30,630 --> 00:33:34,070
And then after that, you can just import deboss.

309
00:33:34,080 --> 00:33:38,950
And then you say you have a function you want to be a workflow.

310
00:33:38,950 --> 00:33:50,389
You just decorate it as at deboss.workflow and then within that workflow function you will see what function calls it makes and then you decorate those functions as at deboss.step.

311
00:33:50,399 --> 00:33:56,870
I think my question was more like how do I figure out which functions I should now go and decorate.

312
00:33:56,880 --> 00:33:59,350
All right.

313
00:33:59,350 --> 00:34:04,070
So I think you essentially decorate any functions that will talk to external APIs or talk to the database.

314
00:34:04,080 --> 00:34:08,069
anything that will generate side effects outside of your program.

315
00:34:08,079 --> 00:34:10,790
Thanks, Chan.

316
00:34:10,790 --> 00:34:21,349
So, I'd like to now go into a different section where we talk about maybe the more use cases that we see for durable execution, especially with AI agents.

317
00:34:21,349 --> 00:34:23,589
Before we go there, is there anything else you'd like to add?

318
00:34:23,599 --> 00:34:32,230
Yeah, so actually I like to talk a bit more about workflow recovery and failure recovery when you use in production.

319
00:34:32,230 --> 00:34:40,470
So as we talked earlier, it's true that developers can write checkpointing code manually,

320
00:34:40,470 --> 00:34:49,909
but you want to use a job execution system or a library like deboss or others because you want to have automatic recovery.

321
00:34:49,909 --> 00:35:03,589
So give you a concrete example in production you usually you have to deploy your code in multiple if you use Kubernetes you will deploy to multiple containers or pods and in a large production development you could probably develop maybe a 100

322
00:35:03,599 --> 00:35:10,950
or a thousand pods that each will serve well you will load balance between those pods to serve requests.

323
00:35:10,950 --> 00:35:17,109
So the problem is that at any point in time or within any hour some pods will definitely fail.

324
00:35:17,119 --> 00:35:21,829
So it's a probability to fail is high when you have a large deployment.

325
00:35:21,829 --> 00:35:28,150
And if you already use Kubernetes, you may think yes, I can just restart those pods.

326
00:35:28,150 --> 00:35:37,910
But the problem is that sure when you restart a pod, you still need some application logic to decide how to resume the work you've done before before you crash.

327
00:35:37,910 --> 00:35:40,150
And if you use deboss, we provide a service called conductor.

328
00:35:40,160 --> 00:35:50,550
So this service basically connects to your running applications and then it will detect when some of the workers are failing.

329
00:35:50,550 --> 00:35:57,910
So if it detects some workers failed, it will try to redistribute the pending workflows running on that worker to other healthy workers.

330
00:35:57,910 --> 00:36:05,109
And this way we automatically recover from failures and we guarantee that all workflows will eventually run to completion.

331
00:36:05,109 --> 00:36:15,910
So I think this is one reason that you really want to delegate this kind kind of recovery to some library or services like deboss.

332
00:36:15,920 --> 00:36:21,030
How does that coexist with the recovery mechanisms in Kubernetes?

333
00:36:21,040 --> 00:36:23,270
Yeah.

334
00:36:23,270 --> 00:36:29,910
So in Kubernetes basically when you restart it will restart your application but it doesn't have any application semantics.

335
00:36:29,910 --> 00:36:31,829
It doesn't know what workflows failed before.

336
00:36:31,839 --> 00:36:40,550
Essentially deboss as a library will have some background threads to listen for tasks say you have to recover this and that.

337
00:36:40,550 --> 00:36:51,589
So deboss will dispatch certain recovery commands to your workers and this works perfectly with other mechanisms in like say kubernetes or other deployments.

338
00:36:51,589 --> 00:37:05,349
So what you mean is that I develop my application with the deboss transact library and then when I deploy it to production I can leverage deboss conductor whether or not I'm deploying to kubernetes.

339
00:37:05,349 --> 00:37:08,069
Yes, exactly.

340
00:37:08,069 --> 00:37:10,790
Do you use deboss conductor automatically in the deboss cloud?

341
00:37:10,800 --> 00:37:14,630
Yes, we have the same capability in deboss cloud.

342
00:37:14,630 --> 00:37:19,430
So if you deploy to deboss cloud you have all the automatic recovery plus autoscaling.

343
00:37:19,430 --> 00:37:22,790
All right, let's spend some time on AI agents.

344
00:37:22,790 --> 00:37:25,990
That was one big use case on your website.

345
00:37:26,000 --> 00:37:36,069
Why is the problem surfacing now with AI agents and requiring durable executions specifically for that?

346
00:37:36,069 --> 00:37:40,790
So I think this is a really new and emerging use case for deboss.

347
00:37:40,790 --> 00:37:45,670
So actually besides AI agents, we also have another AI use case which is AI data pipeline.

348
00:37:45,670 --> 00:37:48,150
If we have time we can dive into that as well.

349
00:37:48,150 --> 00:37:55,750
And for AI agents basically the program will be driven by AI or LLMs.

350
00:37:55,750 --> 00:38:06,390
So say instead of developers coding what functions to call the function calling or tool calling will be decided based on the LLM responses.

351
00:38:06,390 --> 00:38:11,589
So I actually I built a very interesting refund agent uh using langraph and deboss.

352
00:38:11,589 --> 00:38:19,910
So basically what an agent does is that when they receive a refund request, it will talk to LM to decide which tool to use.

353
00:38:19,910 --> 00:38:25,190
Maybe based on the customer record, based on the order, it will call a refund workflow.

354
00:38:25,200 --> 00:38:33,990
So very simplistically, am I right in that the agent is essentially an LLM with tools at the most basic level?

355
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:37,110
Yeah. Okay. Sorry, continue.

356
00:38:37,119 --> 00:38:40,950
Yeah.

357
00:38:40,950 --> 00:38:46,870
So then like the agent can say basically oh if the purchase was above a threshold I will need to send an email

358
00:38:46,880 --> 00:38:58,870
to an admin to verify whether you want to approve this refund or not and then the refund workflow will have to wait for human input and then decide what to do next.

359
00:38:58,870 --> 00:39:03,430
So I would say with AI agent the most challenging part is first dynamic execution of workflows.

360
00:39:03,430 --> 00:39:12,870
you can no longer have a very static workflow like workflow branches or function calling will be very dynamic based on the LLM output

361
00:39:12,870 --> 00:39:26,790
and second it's really unreliable in many ways like from my point of view we treat AI as a unreliable service in the stack so your AI call may fail and then every time you call it it may give

362
00:39:26,790 --> 00:39:38,710
you a different result and then third thing is that human in the loop is really tricky like how do we make AI I work together with human is a kind of new and challenging topic these days.

363
00:39:38,720 --> 00:39:43,109
Can you talk talk more about the agent you developed and how you use deboss there?

364
00:39:43,119 --> 00:39:45,190
Yeah.

365
00:39:45,190 --> 00:39:50,390
So for the agent I developed basically I decorate my tool function as a deboss workflow.

366
00:39:50,390 --> 00:39:56,150
So that it will guarantee basically if I refunded it before I will only refund it once.

367
00:39:56,150 --> 00:40:10,069
So this guarantees that if the user ask I want to refund it again based on the recorded information we'll say you've already been refunded before so we'll skip the refund and they will guarantee that once we kick off the refund workflow it will always finish.

368
00:40:10,069 --> 00:40:19,990
So say if something happened to the program if it crashes in the middle we will just resume the refund process from where it left off.

369
00:40:19,990 --> 00:40:26,470
So like say if I already process the payment or kind of return the money back to user will also restore the inventory and stuff like that.

370
00:40:26,480 --> 00:40:28,870
Basic question though.

371
00:40:28,870 --> 00:40:37,589
So if the agent is the LLM with tool and the LLM is the one calling the tool which in many cases the tool is an API call.

372
00:40:37,589 --> 00:40:42,790
How does now deboss get involved in that part of the workflow?

373
00:40:42,800 --> 00:40:45,910
Yeah, exactly.

374
00:40:45,910 --> 00:40:53,670
So the cool thing is that as I introduced before deboss is a library and annotations that you can wrap around your function with.

375
00:40:53,670 --> 00:40:59,910
So when lm calls the function it will call the wrapped function and that wrap function is a dual workflow.

376
00:40:59,910 --> 00:41:06,790
So that's why it's super easy to add deboss with any AI frameworks.

377
00:41:06,790 --> 00:41:12,390
Like instead of passing your bare function, you pass the annotated function into your AI tools.

378
00:41:12,390 --> 00:41:14,390
That's interesting.

379
00:41:14,390 --> 00:41:17,829
Are you going to talk about data pipelines as well? Is there an example there?

380
00:41:17,839 --> 00:41:21,750
Yeah.

381
00:41:21,750 --> 00:41:32,390
So data pipeline is one like huge use case in deboss where basically the typical pipeline sometimes goes the first is a workflow will first scrape websites

382
00:41:32,390 --> 00:41:45,670
or scrape other data sources and store the PDFs or images into some S3 bucket and then the second step will in parallel we'll use LMS to analyze those images

383
00:41:45,670 --> 00:41:55,991
or PDFs and then the third step will be to persist the return value from LLMs into multiple data stores either vector database or Postgress or you know multiple data sources. Yeah.

384
00:41:56,000 --> 00:42:10,470
And then maybe another step is to do some decision based on or analytics based on the results from previous steps like a concrete use case is for example stock marketing monitoring.

385
00:42:10,470 --> 00:42:23,109
So some of our use cases would be to scrape the website of stock stock markets and then put into LM to some do some analysis and then do some business decision based on like do I want to invest in the stock?

386
00:42:23,119 --> 00:42:26,550
Do I want to sell the stocks as a final step?

387
00:42:26,560 --> 00:42:28,390
Why durable execution?

388
00:42:28,400 --> 00:42:39,030
Yeah, durable execution is essential because you don't want to lose data and once you proc like usually there are two reasons.

389
00:42:39,030 --> 00:42:41,829
One, the data volume is huge, right?

390
00:42:41,829 --> 00:42:49,990
So easily you can process 1,000 documents and if something fell in the middle or not just 1,000 maybe 10,000 documents if something fell in the middle

391
00:42:49,990 --> 00:43:00,390
you don't want to restart from the beginning like you don't want to say oh now something happened to my pipeline either the AI failed or I I hit some AI rate limit.

392
00:43:00,390 --> 00:43:03,589
I don't want to say I have to reprocess all 10,000 documents again.

393
00:43:03,599 --> 00:43:06,470
I want to resume from where it left off.

394
00:43:06,480 --> 00:43:08,230
So that's the first thing.

395
00:43:08,230 --> 00:43:14,069
The second thing is you want everything to complete.

396
00:43:14,069 --> 00:43:18,309
Say if I process 10,000 documents, I want all of them to complete.

397
00:43:18,309 --> 00:43:28,069
Otherwise, my business decision for example, which stock to trade may be based on incomplete data that can cause like financial loss and other sequence consequences.

398
00:43:28,079 --> 00:43:35,109
So in this specific example, if I did decorate the loading step with deboss

399
00:43:35,119 --> 00:43:45,349
and the error was on let's say the 100,000 document in my set of 500,000 what is the behavior?

400
00:43:45,349 --> 00:43:54,951
So basically in this case you want to use deboss cues to paralyze those tasks like you don't want to process those all those documents in one giant step. You want to make sure

401
00:43:54,960 --> 00:44:06,870
that you want to basically say each step is a cued task spawned by my workflow like from documents 1

402
00:44:06,870 --> 00:44:15,109
to 10,000 I will incue a task and then it will become a fan out pattern and then we will wait until all tasks finish.

403
00:44:15,109 --> 00:44:17,910
So I will fan in by waiting those results.

404
00:44:17,910 --> 00:44:23,589
So if any of the tasks failed then we'll when we recover the workflow we'll just say okay

405
00:44:23,599 --> 00:44:37,910
now I have to check all those cute tasks and if some tasks failed then we'll restart execution of those tasks but for those tasks that have already finished we'll just get the result from the database and then return it.

406
00:44:37,910 --> 00:44:42,870
So that's how you can correctly recover from those failures.

407
00:44:42,870 --> 00:44:49,670
So you had earlier mentioned about using conductor for deboss in production.

408
00:44:49,670 --> 00:44:53,670
Besides that, are there other recommendations about being able to run deboss at scale?

409
00:44:53,680 --> 00:44:56,390
Yeah.

410
00:44:56,390 --> 00:44:59,910
So the question is mostly about like the best practices for using deboss.

411
00:44:59,920 --> 00:45:02,871
Mhm. Yeah.

412
00:45:02,871 --> 00:45:08,710
So I think to run deboss at scale, you first really want to make sure that your workflows are deterministic.

413
00:45:08,710 --> 00:45:23,430
That's the core to guarantee your workflows are recoverable because if you're you have nondeterminism in your workflow when we recover it we may go through another execution path that will we don't really know how to recover.

414
00:45:23,430 --> 00:45:27,030
And then the second thing is use cues wisely.

415
00:45:27,030 --> 00:45:34,470
So for example when you deal with LLMs they typically have rate limiting and they also have concurrency limits.

416
00:45:34,470 --> 00:45:38,550
So how many outstanding requests you can have to those LLMs.

417
00:45:38,550 --> 00:45:40,950
So in this case use deboss cues.

418
00:45:40,950 --> 00:45:44,550
So with cues you can add ray limiter.

419
00:45:44,550 --> 00:45:54,870
Say I want at most five API calls uh within like 30 seconds and I want at most 10 outstanding requests at a time.

420
00:45:54,870 --> 00:46:01,750
So with those primitives you can build your apps with parallel task but also within the limit of those LLMs.

421
00:46:01,750 --> 00:46:08,150
Is there anything else you'd like to add for folks that would like to get started with deboss transact?

422
00:46:08,160 --> 00:46:10,870
Yeah.

423
00:46:10,870 --> 00:46:14,790
So for deboss transact, download it, install it, run it locally on your laptop.

424
00:46:14,790 --> 00:46:20,150
The cool thing is that because it's a library, we can also leverage the debugger.

425
00:46:20,150 --> 00:46:31,349
So we actually develop a time travel debugger that you can install from VS Code marketplace where you will be able to replay traces happened in anything happened in the past.

426
00:46:31,349 --> 00:46:40,470
say I want to investigate why the workflow executed that way like executed yesterday then I will just pick the workflow

427
00:46:40,470 --> 00:46:54,069
and then tell our debugger extension to say I want to re-execute that and when we reexecute instead of writing to the database we'll just pull the information from the database say this is the workflow this was the input and those were the output of each step

428
00:46:54,079 --> 00:47:04,950
so we can instead of actually so say if your workflow contains sending email or calling out to stripe we will not actually call those external APIs will instead return the recorded information

429
00:47:04,960 --> 00:47:11,191
and then you'll be able to step through your workflow as if it happened in the past. So that's very cool.

