00:47:11,191 --> 00:47:14,470
Thanks so much for today Chan.

431
00:47:14,470 --> 00:47:16,230
I think a lot of the goal of the conversation,

432
00:47:16,230 --> 00:47:25,190
at least what I was trying to achieve was distinguish between workflow as a graphical representation aid

433
00:47:25,190 --> 00:47:39,270
for business users versus grouping together the series of steps or services that need to be durable and how does that play into the

434
00:47:39,270 --> 00:47:48,550
now where a lot of the code generation is happening with AI as um where exactly does deboss fit into it?

435
00:47:48,550 --> 00:47:56,309
You've explained that you can definitely use the simplicity of deboss and the performance of deboss in grouping together steps

436
00:47:56,309 --> 00:48:09,910
that you need as a workflow and I presume one would be able to use AI to then introspect that code and generate the graphical representation as required for business users.

437
00:48:09,910 --> 00:48:19,190
But then the question would be why would you not use AI to even write all the framework that comes with deboss.

438
00:48:19,190 --> 00:48:25,191
Could you just maybe spend a few minutes on your thinking about how AI fits into all this? Yeah.

439
00:48:25,191 --> 00:48:31,109
So it's a really interesting question and I've been thinking a lot about it recently especially with AI.

440
00:48:31,109 --> 00:48:33,030
We've seen a lot of AI generated code.

441
00:48:33,030 --> 00:48:47,910
It's true that someone may say okay AI may be able to generate those checkpointing code but AI won't be able to generate automatic workflow recovery it won't be able to generate the control plane that can automatically recover on failure so we think deboss

442
00:48:47,910 --> 00:48:56,390
and other workflow engines still played an essential role here in the AI era right and then with AI generated code

443
00:48:56,390 --> 00:49:08,069
I think we really want to make sure they are reliable and by reliable I mean like yes those codes may be buggy so You want to have a way to say if I caught a bug, I want to be able to investigate a bug.

444
00:49:08,069 --> 00:49:13,510
I want to be able to say restart my workflow from a specific step and then fix the bug.

445
00:49:13,510 --> 00:49:17,910
So those are the capabilities where those durable execution engines really shine

446
00:49:17,920 --> 00:49:29,750
because with those AI generated unreliable code like you want a way to correctly store those information for debugging

447
00:49:29,750 --> 00:49:39,910
and because I think the one advantage of deboss is that because we store those like what step has executed and at when the time stamp of it in a relational database.

448
00:49:39,910 --> 00:49:44,549
So we we really have this structured data for AI to analyze.

449
00:49:44,549 --> 00:49:50,309
It's easy for human to analyze and it will also be easy for AI to analyze what's going wrong.

450
00:49:50,309 --> 00:50:01,589
So I think it's a really exciting new capability that we are exploring and I think simplicity and structured data logging are important.

451
00:50:01,589 --> 00:50:09,990
So simplicity is that we actually try to add a prompt for deboss and we give the prompt that will incorporate our latest version of the code.

452
00:50:09,990 --> 00:50:13,990
We'll basically give AI instructions on how to add deboss to your code.

453
00:50:13,990 --> 00:50:18,710
So with that we can just tell AI make this code durable and then AI will correctly do that in one shot.

454
00:50:18,710 --> 00:50:29,349
And that's really interesting because if we put dual execution as a library, we will be able to make AI generated code durable very easily.

455
00:50:29,349 --> 00:50:33,430
And then when you execute that code, we automatically checkpoint data in a database.

456
00:50:33,430 --> 00:50:37,990
So it will be very easy for a human or for AI to investigate what's going on.

457
00:50:37,990 --> 00:50:45,430
And then because everything is a database, it also allows users or AI to automatically fix those code.

458
00:50:45,430 --> 00:50:57,510
because to fix those code you just need several SQL statements to modify the table to change the result to change the output of the recorded outputs table and then to continue from where you left off.

